## The problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first.** Every tenant writes the day first, so
`02/12` (2 December) comes out as 12 February. Every tenant, every way in. This is the fix that is queued.

**`hint`: the booking-v3 form tells people to type the month first.** It prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under
its date field, then reads the date the tenant's way. People do what the hint says and the date
flips. Only reports through the form of a booking-v3 tenant. The parser fix does not touch it: the
hint has to change.

**`shift`: the date moves by one day.** Not a swap: Tuesday 12 May shows as Monday 11 May, a client
in Lisbon gets Tuesday while one in Toronto gets Monday, an invite arrives before the booking. That
is a timezone conversion, not the parser and not the form. The desk had already read these as a
one-day move (`desk-notes.json`) but filed them under `parser` by tenant.

## How this was counted

- One id, one report: the export pages overlap, so repeats are dropped before counting.
- The cause comes from the tenant and the way in (`#lib/kb/causes.ts`), not from the intake tag.
  Intake guesses from the reporter's browser, and the guess is often wrong.
- One-day moves are split out first, from the desk's own notes.
- Every number on this page is computed from the export when the page renders. When the desk
  merges more reports, the table follows.

## Why it was one ticket

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges reports whose subject lines match,
without reading the body. Every report here has a subject like "Date is wrong", so different bugs
ended up in one thread. Its own consequences say engineering would split them later; this page is that split.

